export const HC6={title:'Designing the display board',hook:'Judges spend only a few minutes in front of each board, and classmates walking past may spend only seconds. A great display grabs attention from across the room, guides the eye through the career story and rewards a closer look with accurate detail. Round Two awards 30 points for artistic design, creativity and organization alone.',objectives:['Apply design principles—contrast, alignment, repetition and proximity—to a tri-fold board.','Plan a logical layout across the three panels.','Choose color, fonts and images that support the career theme.','Add creative, safe three-dimensional elements.'],sections:[
['What judges look for',[
'Round Two’s **display visuals** section scores three items at 10 points each. **Artistic design** asks whether the display is visually attractive and well designed. **Creativity and originality** asks whether it is original and creative. **Organization** asks whether the content is clear, concise and well organized.',
'In Round One, the **creative and artistic impact** slides (up to two) let you explain your design choices, and the board photo is scored for showing the entire display clearly.',
'In short, the board must be beautiful, original and easy to follow—all at once.',
flip('Display visuals scoring',[['Artistic design','Visually attractive, well designed (10)'],['Creativity and originality','Original and creative (10)'],['Organization','Clear, concise, well organized (10)'],['Creative impact slides','Explain design choices in Round One'],['Board photo','Whole display shown clearly'],['Theme','Ties design and content together']])]],
['Planning the three panels',[
'A tri-fold has a **center panel** and two **side panels**. Most viewers read **left to right** and look at the **center first**. A common plan: the **center** holds the career title, theme and a striking image or overview; the **left** panel covers responsibilities and work settings; the **right** panel covers education, salary, outlook, benefits and challenges. Additional information and interview quotes can fill remaining space.',
'Sketch the layout on paper at scale before cutting anything. Mark where headings, text blocks, images and 3D items will go, and leave **white space**—crowded boards are hard to read.',
'Follow a clear reading path so a viewer can understand the career without you.',
"Many teams build a small paper mock-up of the board first, folding a sheet of paper into three panels and sketching each section. This quick model lets both members agree on the layout before spending money on printing.",
order('Building the board',['Sketch the layout at scale','Write and edit concise text','Print and mount headings, text and images','Arrange pieces before gluing','Attach securely and check alignment','Add safe 3D items and final touches'])]],
['Design principles',[
'Four principles—often remembered as **CRAP**—make displays look professional. **Contrast** makes important items stand out (dark text on a light background, large headings). **Repetition** reuses colors, fonts and shapes to unify the board. **Alignment** lines items up along invisible edges so nothing looks randomly placed. **Proximity** groups related items together, such as an education heading with its timeline.',
'Establish a **visual hierarchy**: the title is largest, section headings next and body text smallest. Viewers should be able to scan headings in seconds.',
'Apply the same principles to your digital outline slides for a consistent look.',
"Squint at the board from across the room. The elements you can still see—usually the title, headings and large images—are your visual hierarchy, and they should tell the career story even without the small text.",
match('Design principle and example',[['Contrast','Dark text on a light background'],['Repetition','Same heading font and color on every panel'],['Alignment','Text blocks lined up along a common edge'],['Proximity','Salary chart placed next to the salary heading'],['Hierarchy','Title largest, headings next, body smallest'],['White space','Empty space that keeps the board readable']])]],
['Color, fonts and readability',[
'Choose a **limited palette**—two or three main colors plus a neutral—linked to your theme (blues and whites for respiratory therapy, greens for environmental health). Make sure text contrasts strongly with its background; avoid bright text on bright backgrounds.',
'Use **simple, readable fonts**: a bold sans-serif for headings and a clean font for body text. A common guide is that the title should be readable from about 10 feet away and body text from about 3 feet. Limit yourself to two font families.',
'Keep text short: bullet points and short phrases, not paragraphs.',
"Test colors in print, not just on screen. Printed colors often look darker or duller than they do on a monitor, so print a sample heading and body text before producing the final pieces.",
sort('Readable choice or poor choice?',['Readable','Poor'],[['Bold sans-serif headings',0],['Yellow text on a white board',1],['Two coordinated font families',0],['Six different decorative fonts',1],['Short bullet points',0],['Long paragraphs in tiny print',1]])]],
['Images and creative elements',[
'Images make unfamiliar careers concrete—equipment, settings and professionals at work. Use **original photos** (your own job-shadow photos with permission) or images you have the right to use, and caption them. Charts, timelines, icons and diagrams turn data into visuals.',
'**Three-dimensional elements** add creativity: a model lung, a mock lab tray, a sample scrubs pocket with tools, a lift-the-flap panel or a pocket of take-away career cards. All must be safe, secure and within the size limits, with no electricity.',
'Creativity should **support understanding**, not distract from it.',
"Interactive features such as flaps, pull tabs or small booklets invite viewers to engage, but keep them sturdy and simple. Judges and visitors will handle them many times during display time.",
chk('Which 3D element best supports a respiratory therapy display?',['A model of the lungs showing where treatments act','A battery-powered fan','A live plant','Glitter covering the text'],'Relevant, safe and power-free.')]],
['Construction quality',[
'A well-built board looks professional. Use the required **foam board or corrugated cardboard** tri-fold. Print text cleanly, trim edges straight and mount pieces on colored backing for contrast. Use strong adhesive so items do not fall off during transport; secure 3D items so judges can handle them.',
'Check spelling and grammar carefully—errors are very visible on a board. Pack the display carefully for travel, with a repair kit (tape, glue dots, extra copies of text).',
'Write **team information on the back** of the board as the guidelines require.',
ex('Readability check','Test whether your board can be read at the expected distances.',['Stand about 10 feet away: can you read the title and theme?','Stand about 3 feet away: can you read body text easily?','Ask a classmate to find the education section in five seconds','Fix anything that fails'],'A board that passes all three checks is organized and readable.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Design review',{a:{text:'Your draft board has six fonts, long paragraphs and no images. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change first?',choices:[['Reduce to two fonts, cut text to bullets and add relevant images','b','Correct.',true],['Add more paragraphs','b','Less text reads better.',false]]},b:{text:'You want a creative element. Which fits the rules?',choices:[['A secure lift-the-flap panel revealing career facts','c','Correct.',true],['A light-up sign with batteries','c','No power is allowed.',false]]},c:{text:'Your board is readable, creative and within the rules.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Display visuals earn 30 points: artistic design, creativity and originality, and organization; Round One’s creative impact slides explain your design choices.','Plan the panels with the center for title and theme, sides for responsibilities and education or salary; sketch at scale and leave white space; apply contrast, repetition, alignment, proximity and visual hierarchy.','Use a limited color palette, two readable fonts (title readable at about 10 feet), short text, legal images and safe, relevant 3D elements; build carefully and write team information on the back.'],terms:[['Visual hierarchy','Arranging elements by size and emphasis so the most important stands out.'],['White space','Empty space that improves readability.'],['Proximity','Grouping related items close together.']],mcqs:[q('How many points does Round Two give for display visuals?','30','10','5','50','Three items at 10.'),q('Which principle groups related items together?','Proximity','Contrast','Repetition','Hierarchy','Closeness.'),q('Which principle reuses colors and fonts?','Repetition','Proximity','Alignment','Contrast','Unity.'),q('Where do viewers usually look first on a tri-fold?','The center panel','The back','The table','The floor','Focal point.'),q('About how far away should the title be readable?','About 10 feet','About 1 inch','About 100 feet','Only up close','Readability guide.'),q('How many font families should you generally use?','About two','Six or more','One per word','None','Consistency.'),q('Which 3D item is allowed?','A secure model lung','A battery-powered display','A live animal','A real needle','Safe and power-free.'),q('Where must team information be written?','On the back of the board','On the front title','Nowhere','On the floor','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would design a tri-fold board that scores well for artistic design, creativity and organization.',rubric:[['Layout',['center','panels','left','right','sketch','white space','reading path']],['Principles',['contrast','repetition','alignment','proximity','hierarchy']],['Color, fonts and images',['palette','fonts','readable','10 feet','images','charts','captions']],['Creativity and construction',['3d','model','safe','no electricity','secure','spelling','back']]],model:'For a respiratory therapy display themed “Every breath matters,” we would first sketch the board at scale. The center panel would hold the large title, the theme and a striking original photo of a therapist adjusting a ventilator, along with a short career overview. The left panel would show job responsibilities as icons with short labels and a day-in-the-life timeline, and the right panel would show the education road map, a labeled salary chart and a two-column benefits-and-challenges section, leaving white space so the board does not feel crowded. We would apply contrast with dark navy text on white backing, repetition by using the same heading font and blue accents on every panel, alignment by lining text blocks along common edges and proximity by placing each chart beside its heading, with a clear hierarchy from title to headings to body text. We would limit ourselves to two fonts, keep text to bullet points and test that the title can be read from about 10 feet away. For creativity, we would add a secure model of the lungs showing where inhaled medications act and a lift-the-flap panel with surprising facts, all within the 48-by-24-by-36-inch footprint and with no electricity or batteries. Finally, we would mount pieces neatly with strong adhesive, proofread every word, pack a repair kit and write our team information on the back of the board.'},sources:[RULES,PURDUE]};

export const HC7={title:'Display rules, safety and copyright',hook:'A beautiful board can still lose points—or be disqualified—if it breaks the rules. Round Two’s display overview checks three things before judges even consider your design: no power, safety and size. Copyright rules apply to every image and quote. This lesson makes sure your display is fully compliant.',objectives:['Apply the size and material requirements for the display.','Identify items that are not allowed for safety reasons.','Use images and content legally and give credit.','Run a compliance check before competition.'],sections:[
['Size and materials',[
'The display is a **tri-fold board 36″ high × 48″ wide**, made of **foam or corrugated cardboard**, with optional models and materials. Everything—the board plus any items—must fit within **48″ wide × 24″ deep × 36″ high** on the table. Items cannot extend beyond these dimensions.',
'Measure your completed display, including 3D items, with a tape measure. Items placed in front of the board count toward the 24-inch depth.',
'The display overview scores **size** at 5 points.',
"Measure early in the design process, not just at the end. Sketching the display footprint on a table with tape helps you plan where models can sit without crossing the depth limit.",
sort('Within the rules?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['36″ × 48″ foam tri-fold',0],['A 60″-wide banner',1],['A model that sticks 30″ in front of the board',1],['Corrugated cardboard tri-fold',0],['Small models within the 24″ depth',0]])]],
['No power of any kind',[
'The display may **not** use **electricity**, **batteries** or **electronic devices** of any kind—no tablets, lights, sound modules, fans or plug-in items. This applies even to small battery-operated features.',
'If you want to show something electronic, use a printed screenshot, a model or a mock-up instead. For example, print an ECG strip, a mock electronic health record screen or a photo of an ultrasound image.',
'The display overview scores **no power** at 5 points.',
"Remember that the rule covers anything with a power source, including items that might seem harmless, such as greeting-card sound chips, small clocks or toys with batteries. Remove batteries entirely rather than simply turning a device off.",
chk('You want to show an ultrasound image. What is allowed?',['A printed image or mock-up','A tablet playing a video','A battery-powered screen','A plug-in monitor'],'No electricity or batteries.')]],
['Safety rules',[
'For safety, displays may **not** include **open flames**, **body fluids**, **living organisms** (plants, animals, microbes), **sharps** (needles, scalpels, lancets) or **invasive items**. Chemicals and hazardous materials should also be avoided.',
'Replace real items with safe substitutes: a capped, needle-free syringe model or a photo instead of a real needle; colored water substitutes are risky too, so a printed image of blood tubes is safer. Items judges may handle should be sturdy and clean.',
'The display overview scores **safety** at 5 points; an unsafe item can also cause removal from the display area.',
"Medical props deserve special care. Even an unused, capped needle counts as a sharp, and items such as tourniquets or catheters may be considered invasive. When in doubt, use a photograph or a clearly labeled model made from paper or plastic.",
sort('Safe to include?',['Safe to include','Not allowed'],[['Printed photo of blood collection tubes',0],['A real needle',1],['A plastic model of the heart',0],['A live plant',1],['A candle',1],['A printed mock patient chart (fictional)',0]])]],
['Copyright and permission',[
'All work must be the **original work** of the team, and **clip art and photos must follow copyright law**. Copyright protects creative works such as photos, drawings and text as soon as they are created. Using someone else’s image without permission can violate copyright, even if it appears on a website.',
'Safe options include **your own photos** (with permission from people pictured and from the facility), **public domain** images, images with **Creative Commons** licenses that allow your use (with attribution), and images from sources that explicitly permit educational use. Credit every image source on the reference slides.',
'The Round One rubric scores **originality** at 5 points.',
"Keep a simple log of every image: where it came from, its license or permission and how you credited it. This log makes building the reference slides much easier and protects your team if a judge asks about a source.",
match('Image source and how to use it',[['Your own job-shadow photo','Get permission from people and the facility'],['Creative Commons image','Follow the license and give attribution'],['Public domain image','Free to use; credit the source'],['Random web image','Do not use without permission'],['Government data chart','Usually public domain; cite it'],['Original drawing','Your own work; strongest originality']])]],
['Privacy and professionalism',[
'Never include real **patient information**—names, photos or records. If an interviewee shares a patient story, remove identifying details. Get permission before photographing professionals or facilities, and follow any facility photo policies.',
'Keep content professional and accurate: no exaggerated claims, stereotypes or unsupported statistics. Label invented examples, such as a mock patient chart, as fictional.',
'Professional content reflects well on your chapter and HOSA.',
"If you create a mock document, such as a sample patient chart or lab report, fill it with obviously fictional information and label it “sample” so no one mistakes it for a real record.",
chk('A therapist gives you a photo of a patient on a ventilator. What do you do?',['Do not use it unless proper patient consent exists; use an equipment-only photo instead','Use it with the patient’s name','Post it anyway because the therapist gave it to you','Blur it slightly and use it'],'Patient privacy comes first.')]],
['The compliance checklist',[
'Before competition, check every rule: the career is a health career; the board is a 36″ × 48″ tri-fold of foam or cardboard; the whole display fits within 48″ × 24″ × 36″; no electricity, batteries or electronics; no flames, fluids, living organisms, sharps or invasive items; all images are original or legally used and credited; team information is on the back; and the outline photo matches the final display.',
'Bring the checklist to setup and recheck after travel, since items can shift or break.',
"Assign one member to own the checklist and the other to verify it independently. Two sets of eyes catch problems, such as a forgotten battery or an uncredited image, that one person may miss.",
"Keep photos of the display and the checklist on your phone. If anything is damaged in transit, the photos help you rebuild sections quickly and accurately during setup.",
order('Compliance check before ILC',['Confirm the career is a health career','Measure the entire display','Remove anything with power','Remove unsafe items','Verify image permissions and credits','Write team information on the back','Recheck after transport'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Compliance problems',{a:{text:'A teammate wants to tape a small LED light behind the title. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you say?',choices:[['No—batteries and lights are not allowed; use contrast or reflective paper instead','b','Correct.',true],['Yes, it is tiny','b','Any power is prohibited.',false]]},b:{text:'You found a perfect photo on a news website. Can you use it?',choices:[['Only with permission or a license allowing use; otherwise find a legal alternative','c','Correct.',true],['Yes, it is online','c','Online does not mean free to use.',false]]},c:{text:'Your display passes every compliance check.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The display is a 36″ × 48″ foam or corrugated cardboard tri-fold; everything must fit within 48″ W × 24″ D × 36″ H; no electricity, batteries or electronics.','Safety: no flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps or invasive items—use printed images and models instead; the display overview scores no power, safety and size (5 each).','All work must be original; clip art and photos must follow copyright law (own photos with permission, public domain, Creative Commons with attribution); protect patient privacy and run a compliance checklist.'],terms:[['Copyright','Legal protection for creative works such as photos and text.'],['Creative Commons','Licenses that let creators allow certain uses of their work.'],['Public domain','Works not protected by copyright that anyone may use.']],mcqs:[q('What is the maximum depth of the display?','24 inches','48 inches','12 inches','36 inches','Footprint.'),q('Which material is allowed for the tri-fold?','Foam or corrugated cardboard','Glass','Metal sheets','Wood panels only','Rule.'),q('Which item is allowed?','A printed photo of blood tubes','A real needle','A candle','A live fish','Safe substitute.'),q('Which is prohibited?','A battery-powered light','A paper model','A printed chart','A cardboard pocket','No power.'),q('An image found on a website is:','Not automatically free to use','Always public domain','Always yours','Never copyrighted','Copyright.'),q('Creative Commons images require you to:','Follow the license and give attribution','Pay a fee always','Hide the source','Change the colors','License terms.'),q('Which three items are scored in the display overview?','No power, safety and size','Color, font and glue','Price, weight and age','Votes, likes and shares','5 points each.'),q('Patient photos should be:','Avoided unless proper consent exists','Used with names','Posted freely','Edited slightly','Privacy.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the size, power, safety and copyright rules for the Health Career Display and how your team would make sure your display complies.',rubric:[['Size and materials',['36','48','tri-fold','foam','cardboard','24','depth']],['Power and safety',['no electricity','batteries','electronic','flames','fluids','living','sharps']],['Copyright',['original','copyright','permission','public domain','creative commons','attribution','credit']],['Compliance',['checklist','measure','privacy','back','recheck']]],model:'Our display must be a 36-inch-high by 48-inch-wide tri-fold made of foam or corrugated cardboard, and everything, including any models, must fit within 48 inches wide, 24 inches deep and 36 inches high on the table, so we would measure the finished display with all 3D items in place. No electricity, batteries or electronic devices of any kind are allowed, so instead of a screen showing an ultrasound, we would use printed images and mock-ups. For safety, we cannot include open flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps or invasive items, so we would use photos of blood collection tubes and a plastic heart model instead of real items, making sure anything judges may handle is sturdy and clean. All work must be our own, and clip art and photos must follow copyright law; we would use our own job-shadow photos with permission from the people pictured and the facility, public domain government images and Creative Commons images whose licenses allow our use, crediting every source on our reference slides. We would never show real patient information. Before ILC, we would run a checklist: confirm the career is a health career, measure the display, remove anything with power or safety risks, verify image permissions and credits, confirm our team information is written on the back and recheck everything after transport.'},sources:[RULES,COPY,CC]};

export const HC8={title:'Building the digital outline',hook:'Round One is worth 110 points, and judges see only your digital outline—no display, no presentation. The outline is a slide-style plan of your board that must cover every required section, include a clear photo of the finished display and stay within 16 slides plus references. A careful outline earns many of the easiest points in the event.',objectives:['Build each required slide with the information judges score.','Stay within slide limits and the 16-slide total.','Photograph the finished display clearly.','Format references and submit one PDF on time.'],sections:[
['Required slides and limits',[
'The outline must follow a set structure. The **title slide** (1) lists the event, team member names, division, chapter number, school name and chartered association. Then come the **career overview** (up to 2), **job responsibilities** (up to 2), **educational requirements** (up to 2), **entry-level salary** at local, national and global levels (up to 2), **benefits and challenges** (up to 2), **additional relevant information** (up to 2) and **creative and artistic impact** (up to 2), followed by **one photo** of the completed display (1) and **reference slides**.',
'The outline may include **no more than 16 slides**, not counting reference slides. Count carefully—the “total slides” item is scored.',
{t:'table',caption:'Outline structure (Round One)',head:['Section','Max slides','Points'],rows:[['Health career (whole outline)','—','5'],['Title','1','5'],['Career overview','2','10'],['Job responsibilities','2','10'],['Educational requirements','2','10'],['Entry-level salary','2','10'],['Benefits and challenges','2','10'],['Additional information','2','10'],['Creative and artistic impact','2','10'],['Photo of display','1','10'],['References','—','5']]}]],
['Content slides',[
'Each content slide should present **accurate, current, logically organized** information. The **overview** explains what the career is and why it matters. **Responsibilities** list five or more specific tasks. **Education** details degrees, courses, certificates, licenses and estimated time. **Salary** gives entry-level figures at three levels with sources. **Benefits and challenges** are balanced. **Additional information** may include outlook, specialties, work settings, professional organizations or technology trends.',
'Use concise bullets, consistent headings and visuals such as charts and timelines. Judges read the outline without you, so each slide must make sense on its own.',
'Keep a clear connection between slides and board panels.',
"Use the slide notes or a planning document to track which source supports each fact on each slide. This makes it easy to confirm accuracy before submission and to answer judges’ questions later.",
match('Slide and what it must show',[['Title','Event, names, division, chapter #, school, association'],['Job responsibilities','Five or more specific tasks'],['Education','Degrees, credentials and time to complete'],['Salary','Entry-level local, national and global'],['Benefits and challenges','Balanced rewards and difficulties'],['Creative impact','How design choices communicate the career']])]],
['The creative and artistic impact slides',[
'These slides explain **how and why** you designed the board. Describe your **theme**, **color choices** and what they represent, the **layout** and reading path, **3D elements** and what they teach, and how design helps viewers understand the career.',
'Include small images of design details, such as a close-up of a model or a lift-the-flap panel. Explain creativity in terms of communication: “The lung model shows viewers where inhaled medications act, which is the core of what respiratory therapists do.”',
'This section is worth 10 points and connects Round One to the display visuals scored in Round Two.',
chk('What should the creative impact slides explain?',['How design choices help communicate the career','Only the cost of supplies','The team’s favorite colors without reasons','Nothing—they are optional decoration'],'Design with purpose.')]],
['Photographing the display',[
'The outline must include **one picture of the completed display**, and it must **clearly show the entire display**. Photograph the board straight on in good, even lighting against a plain background, with all three panels and 3D items visible. Avoid glare, shadows and people blocking the view.',
'Take several shots and choose the sharpest. Make sure the board in the photo is the **final version** you will bring to competition, since judges compare them.',
'Close-up detail images belong in the creative impact slides, not in place of the full photo.',
"A phone camera works well if you hold it steady at the center of the board, at roughly the height of the middle panel. Turn off digital filters and crop only the background, not the edges of the display.",
order('Taking the display photo',['Finish the board completely','Set it on a table against a plain background','Use bright, even lighting without glare','Photograph straight on with all panels visible','Take several shots and choose the sharpest','Insert one full-display photo in the outline'])]],
['References and organization',[
'**Reference slides** list every source in **APA** format. They do not count toward the 16 slides. A clean, consistent reference list earns the 5-point references item. Include sources for images as well as facts.',
'The **organization** item rewards an outline that is logical, consistent and easy to follow: same fonts and colors, clear headings, no spelling errors and slides in the required order.',
'Proofread out loud and ask an advisor or classmate to review.',
"Read the outline once as if you were a judge with the rating sheet beside you. Check off each rubric item as you find it; anything you cannot quickly find needs to be clearer.",
sort('Counts toward 16 slides?',['Counts','Does not count'],[['Title slide',0],['Career overview slides',0],['Reference slides',1],['Photo of the display',0],['Creative impact slides',0]])]],
['Submitting the PDF',[
'One team member uploads the outline as **one PDF document** by **May 15** for ILC, following any separate state deadlines and processes. Export from your slide software as a PDF and open it to check that images, fonts and pages appear correctly.',
'Check the file name, page order and slide count before uploading. Submit early to avoid last-minute technical problems, and save a backup copy.',
'Late or incorrect submissions can cost the team its chance to compete.',
ex('Slide count check','Your outline has 1 title, 2 overview, 2 responsibilities, 2 education, 2 salary, 2 benefits/challenges, 2 additional information, 2 creative impact, 1 photo and 2 reference slides. Is it within the limit?',['Count non-reference slides: 1+2+2+2+2+2+2+2+1','Total = 16','Reference slides do not count','16 ≤ 16'],'Yes—16 slides plus 2 reference slides is within the limit.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Outline review',{a:{text:'Your outline has 18 slides, not counting references. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Combine or trim slides to reach 16 or fewer','b','Correct.',true],['Submit anyway','b','The total-slides item is scored.',false]]},b:{text:'Your display photo shows only the center panel up close. What is wrong?',choices:[['The photo must clearly show the entire display; use close-ups only in the creative slides','c','Correct.',true],['Nothing','c','The whole display must be visible.',false]]},c:{text:'You upload one clean PDF well before May 15.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Round One (110 points) judges only the outline: title (1 slide), overview, responsibilities, education, salary, benefits and challenges, additional information and creative impact (up to 2 each), one full-display photo and APA references; no more than 16 slides excluding references.','Each slide must stand alone with accurate, current, concise information; creative impact slides explain how design choices communicate the career.','Photograph the entire final display clearly in even light; proofread for organization; one member uploads one PDF by May 15 after checking slide count and pages.'],terms:[['Digital outline','A slide-style plan of the display judged in Round One.'],['Title slide','The first slide with event, names, division, chapter, school and association.'],['Reference slide','A slide listing sources in APA format; not counted in the 16.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is Round One worth?','110','115','225','50','Outline.'),q('How many slides may the title use?','1','2','3','Unlimited','Required.'),q('Which slides do not count toward the 16?','Reference slides','Title slide','Photo slide','Overview slides','Excluded.'),q('What must the display photo show?','The entire display clearly','Only the team','Only the title','A close-up of a model','Rubric.'),q('How is the outline submitted?','As one PDF by one member','As many image files','On paper at ILC','By email to judges','Format.'),q('By when must the outline be submitted for ILC?','May 15','January 1','The day of the event','June 30','Deadline.'),q('Which slide explains design choices?','Creative and artistic impact','Salary','Title','References','Design.'),q('Which reference style is used?','APA','None','Emoji','Random','Health science.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would build and submit a digital outline that earns full points in Round One.',rubric:[['Structure',['title','overview','responsibilities','education','salary','benefits','additional','creative','photo','references']],['Limits',['16','two','reference','not count','order']],['Quality',['accurate','current','concise','stand alone','apa','proofread','organization']],['Photo and submission',['entire display','lighting','final','one pdf','may 15','early']]],model:'Our outline would follow the required order. A single title slide would list Health Career Display, both of our names, our division, chapter number, school and chartered association. Then we would use up to two slides each for the career overview, job responsibilities with at least five specific tasks, educational requirements with degrees, credentials and estimated time, entry-level salary at local, national and global levels with sources and dates, balanced benefits and challenges, additional information such as job outlook and specialties, and creative and artistic impact explaining our theme, colors, layout and 3D elements and how they help viewers understand the career. One slide would hold a clear, straight-on photo of the entire finished display in even lighting, followed by APA reference slides for every fact and image. We would count carefully to keep the outline at no more than 16 slides excluding references, combining sections if needed. Each slide would use concise bullets, consistent fonts and colors and visuals such as charts and timelines so it makes sense without us. We would proofread aloud and have our advisor review it, export it as one PDF, open the PDF to check pages and images, and have one member upload it well before May 15, keeping a backup copy.'},sources:[RULES,APA]};

export const HC9={title:'Display time and the presentation',hook:'At ILC, your display becomes a conversation. During the required display time, classmates, judges and visitors stop to ask about your career. Later, the top teams have five minutes to show judges what they know, why they care and how their display tells the story. Content items in Round Two are worth 50 points.',objectives:['Prepare for the Required Project Display Time.','Plan a five-minute presentation covering the scored content items.','Incorporate the display and its items into the presentation.','Explain why your team chose the career and what you learned.'],sections:[
['The Required Project Display Time',[
'All competitors must attend the **Required Project Display Time** at ILC, standing with their display and **sharing career information with others**. Teams that miss it lose **15 points** from Round Two. Check the conference schedule for the exact time and location.',
'Arrive early to set up, check that nothing was damaged in transport and fix problems with your repair kit. Prepare a short **30-second “elevator pitch”** about your career for visitors, and be ready for common questions: What does this professional do? How long does training take? What surprised you?',
'Professional, friendly conversation also helps you rehearse for Round Two.',
"Visitors at display time include students who may be considering the career. Treat each conversation as an opportunity to explain clearly and listen to their questions; this practice also reveals which parts of your explanation need work before Round Two.",
order('Display time routine',['Arrive early and set up','Check for damage and repair','Review your elevator pitch','Greet visitors and share career facts','Answer questions confidently','Pack up carefully afterward'])]],
['What the presentation should cover',[
'The **five-minute presentation** may include **why the team chose the career**, **what they learned through their research** and **what is included on the display and why**. Judges score **understanding of the career** (15), **why this career** (15), **presentation of career information** (15) and **display incorporated into the presentation** (5).',
'A timekeeper holds a card at **one minute remaining** and stops the presentation at **five minutes**. Plan about four and a half minutes so you finish strong.',
'Notecards are allowed; electronic notes are not shown.',
flip('Round Two content items',[['Understanding of the career','Clear, detailed knowledge (15)'],['Why this career','Personal, specific reasons (15)'],['Career information','Accurate, organized, engaging (15)'],['Display incorporated','Point to and use the board (5)'],['Timing','Card at 1 minute; stop at 5'],['Notes','Notecards only']])]],
['Structuring five minutes',[
'A clear structure helps judges follow you. **Opening (about 30 seconds):** a hook—a surprising fact, a short story or a question—plus the career name and theme. **Why we chose it (about 45 seconds):** personal, specific reasons. **Career information (about 2½ minutes):** responsibilities, education, salary and outlook, benefits and challenges, moving across the board. **What we learned (about 45 seconds):** surprises and insights. **Closing (about 30 seconds):** a memorable final line that ties back to the theme.',
'Divide sections between both members with smooth handoffs.',
"Write your plan on notecards with key phrases rather than full sentences. Number the cards and mark where each handoff happens so neither member loses their place under pressure.",
ex('Timing plan','Plan a 5-minute presentation with a safety margin.',['Opening and hook: 0:30','Why we chose the career: 0:45','Career information across the board: 2:30','What we learned: 0:45','Closing: 0:20','Total: 4:50'],'A 4:50 plan leaves a margin before the 5-minute stop.')]],
['Explaining “why this career”',[
'“Why this career” is worth as much as understanding the career. Strong answers are **personal and specific**: a family member’s care by a respiratory therapist, a job shadow that revealed a career you had never heard of, a long-term goal such as working in neonatal intensive care, or a fascination with imaging technology. Weak answers are generic—“because it helps people” or “because it pays well.”',
'Connect your reasons to what you learned: how research confirmed or changed your view of the career.',
'Both members can share their own reasons briefly.',
"Your reasons can also explain how the career fits your strengths, such as enjoying science and technology or staying calm in emergencies, which links the “why” to what you learned about the skills professionals need.",
sort('Strong or weak “why”?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Our grandmother’s COPD care showed us what therapists do',0],['Because it helps people',1],['A job shadow in the ICU sparked our interest',0],['It sounded easy',1],['We want to work with newborns in neonatal care',0],['Our teacher picked it',1]])]],
['Using the display',[
'Judges score how well the display is **incorporated into the presentation**. Move across the board as you speak—**point to** each section, **pick up** display items to demonstrate (presenters may do this) and invite judges to look at specific features. For example: “This model shows where nebulized medication reaches the airways,” or “Our road map shows the two-year path to becoming a therapist.”',
'Do not read the board aloud; use it as a visual aid while you explain in your own words.',
'Practice handling items so movements are smooth and safe.',
"Plan where each member will stand and which items each will pick up. Practice returning items to the board quickly so the display stays tidy for the next section.",
chk('Which best incorporates the display?',['Picking up the lung model to show where treatments act','Reading every word on the board','Standing in front of the board blocking it','Ignoring the board entirely'],'Use the display as an active aid.')]],
['Showing deep understanding',[
'Judges look for understanding beyond the board: explaining **why** tasks matter, how the career fits on the **health care team**, how **technology** is changing it and what **challenges** professionals face. Include insights from your interview or job shadow, such as a quote or a surprising detail.',
'Use correct **medical terminology** and define unfamiliar terms briefly. Be ready to explain any number on your board—where it came from and what it means.',
'Prepare for questions that may come up during display time as well.',
"Judges also notice whether both members understand the career, not just their own sections. Each member should be able to explain any number or fact on the board in their own words.",
chk('Which statement shows deeper understanding?',['Therapists adjust ventilator settings based on blood gas results to protect the lungs','Therapists are nice','The career is in hospitals','We like this career'],'Explains what and why.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Presentation planning',{a:{text:'Your practice run took 6 minutes. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Trim details, keep the key content and rehearse to about 4:45','b','Correct.',true],['Speak faster','b','Rushing hurts delivery.',false]]},b:{text:'Your “why” section says, “We picked it because it helps people.” How do you improve it?',choices:[['Share a specific personal experience and goal','c','Correct.',true],['Leave it as is','c','Specific reasons score higher.',false]]},c:{text:'Your presentation is timed, personal and uses the display throughout.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['All teams must attend the Required Project Display Time (−15 if missed); arrive early, repair damage and prepare a 30-second pitch and answers.','The five-minute presentation covers why you chose the career, what you learned and what is on the display; content items are understanding (15), why this career (15), career information (15) and display incorporated (5); card at one minute; notecards only.','Structure about 4:45 (hook, why, information across the board, what you learned, closing); give personal, specific reasons; point to and pick up display items; show depth with interview insights and correct terms.'],terms:[['Required Project Display Time','A mandatory ILC session where teams share their display.'],['Elevator pitch','A short, clear summary of your career for visitors.'],['Incorporating the display','Using the board and items actively during the presentation.']],mcqs:[q('What is the penalty for missing display time?','15 points','5 points','Disqualification','None','Rule.'),q('How long is the presentation?','5 minutes','3 minutes','10 minutes','7 minutes','Time limit.'),q('When is the time card shown?','At one minute remaining','At the start','At two minutes remaining','Never','Timekeeper.'),q('How many points is “why this career” worth?','15','5','10','25','Content.'),q('Which is a strong reason for choosing a career?','A job shadow in the ICU sparked our interest','It sounded easy','Our teacher picked it','We had no choice','Specific.'),q('May presenters pick up display items?','Yes','No','Only judges may','Only after time is up','Allowed.'),q('Which notes may be used?','Notecards','A tablet shown to judges','A phone','A laptop','Paper only.'),q('How should the board be used?','As a visual aid while explaining in your own words','Read aloud word for word','Hidden','Ignored','Incorporate.')],frq:{prompt:'Plan a five-minute Round Two presentation for your health career that scores well on all content items.',rubric:[['Structure and timing',['hook','why','information','learned','closing','4:45','one minute']],['Why this career',['personal','specific','experience','goal','job shadow']],['Understanding',['responsibilities','education','salary','team','technology','interview','terminology']],['Display',['point','pick up','model','board','visual aid','not read']]],model:'Our five-minute presentation on respiratory therapy would open with a hook: “Every day, you take about 20,000 breaths—and for some patients, a respiratory therapist makes each one possible.” We would introduce the career and our theme, “Every breath matters,” in about 30 seconds. Next, we would each share a specific reason we chose the career—one of us watched a therapist help a grandparent with COPD, and the other became interested in neonatal care during a job shadow—taking about 45 seconds. For about two and a half minutes we would move across the board, pointing to the responsibilities panel and picking up our lung model to show where nebulized medications act, explaining how therapists adjust ventilator settings based on blood gas results, walking through the two-year education road map and credentialing exam, explaining our entry-level salary chart and the reasons for strong job outlook, and discussing balanced benefits and challenges, including night shifts. Then we would spend about 45 seconds on what we learned, such as how closely therapists work with ICU teams, using a quote from the therapist we interviewed. We would close with a line tying back to our theme. We would rehearse to about 4 minutes 45 seconds, use notecards only, hand off smoothly between us and use the board as a visual aid rather than reading it.'},sources:[RULES,EHC]};

export const HC10={title:'Delivery, teamwork and final preparation',hook:'Two teams can present the same facts and earn very different scores. Delivery—voice, stage presence, diction and team participation—makes up 20 points of Round Two, and it shapes how judges hear everything else. This lesson brings together delivery skills, teamwork and a final checklist for competition day.',objectives:['Use voice, stage presence and diction effectively.','Share the presentation equally and transition smoothly.','Rehearse with feedback and manage nerves.','Complete a final competition-day checklist.'],sections:[
['Delivery scoring',[
'Round Two scores four delivery items at **5 points each**. **Voice** checks pitch, tempo, volume and quality. **Stage presence** checks poise, posture, eye contact and enthusiasm. **Diction** checks pronunciation, grammar and articulation. **Team participation** checks that both members take an active role.',
'These 20 points are easy to earn with practice. Combined with the display overview (15), display visuals (30) and content (50), they make up the 115-point Round Two.',
'Remember that missing display time subtracts 15 points from Round Two.',
"Judges often decide delivery scores in the first minute, so a confident, well-practiced opening matters. Both members should look ready and engaged from the moment they enter the room.",
flip('Delivery items',[['Voice','Pitch, tempo, volume, quality'],['Stage presence','Poise, posture, eye contact, enthusiasm'],['Diction','Pronunciation, grammar, articulation'],['Team participation','Both members actively involved'],['Total delivery','20 points'],['Round Two total','115 points']])]],
['Voice and diction',[
'Speak at a **comfortable, clear volume** that reaches the judges without shouting. Vary **pitch** and **pace** to emphasize key points and avoid monotone. **Pause** after important facts so they sink in. Replace filler words (“um,” “like”) with brief pauses.',
'Practice pronouncing **medical terms** correctly—for example, “bronchodilator,” “sonographer,” “phlebotomist.” Use correct grammar and complete sentences, and define terms simply for listeners.',
'Record practice runs and listen for speed, fillers and unclear words.',
"Practice in the kind of room you will present in, if possible. Larger rooms need more volume and slower pacing than a classroom, and background noise from nearby presentations may require extra projection.",
sort('Helps or hurts delivery?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Pausing after key facts',0],['Speaking in a monotone',1],['Pronouncing terms correctly',0],['Frequent “um” and “like”',1],['Varying pace for emphasis',0],['Mumbling while facing the board',1]])]],
['Stage presence',[
'Stand tall with relaxed shoulders and feet planted; avoid swaying or fidgeting. Make **eye contact** with each judge, not just the board or notecards. Use **natural gestures**, especially when pointing to display features. **Smile** and show genuine enthusiasm—judges notice passion for the career.',
'Dress professionally in HOSA official attire or business attire as required. Turn toward the judges when speaking, even when gesturing to the board, so your voice carries and judges see your face.',
'Confidence grows with preparation; practice until the content feels natural.',
"Plan your first and last moments. Walk in calmly, set up efficiently, smile and greet the judges by introducing yourselves; at the end, pause, thank them and wait for any instructions before packing up.",
chk('When pointing to the board, you should:',['Gesture toward it while still facing the judges','Turn your back to the judges','Read the board silently','Stand in front of the section'],'Keep facing the audience.')]],
['Teamwork',[
'Both members must take an **active role**. Divide the presentation so each person presents meaningful content—not one speaker and one helper. For example, one member leads the opening and responsibilities while the other leads education and salary, and both share their own “why.”',
'Plan **smooth handoffs** (“Now Maya will explain how to become a therapist”), stand on opposite sides of the board so neither blocks it and support each other—if one forgets a point, the other can add it naturally.',
'Rehearse together often so transitions feel seamless.',
"Agree in advance on how to handle mistakes. If one member skips a point, the other can add it at the next handoff with a phrase such as “As my partner mentioned, …,” keeping the presentation smooth.",
order('Preparing as a team',['Divide content equally','Write handoff lines','Practice positions beside the board','Rehearse full runs together','Give each other feedback','Rehearse again with timing'])]],
['Rehearsing with feedback',[
'Rehearse the full presentation many times, timed, in front of an audience—classmates, advisors or family. Ask for feedback on clarity, timing, delivery and use of the display. Practice answering questions you might hear during display time.',
'Simulate competition: set up the board, use notecards, have someone hold a one-minute card and stop you at five minutes. Video practice runs to spot habits.',
'Manage nerves with slow breathing, positive self-talk and a well-practiced opening.',
"Schedule rehearsals across several weeks rather than cramming the night before. Spaced practice builds confident recall, and each round of feedback has time to make a visible difference.",
ex('Using a feedback form','A classmate scores a practice run (invented for practice).',['Voice: 4/5—speed up slightly in the middle','Stage presence: 3/5—more eye contact with judges','Diction: 5/5','Team participation: 4/5—balance speaking time','Plan: practice eye contact and swap one section'],'Targeted changes raise delivery scores before competition (scores invented for practice).')]],
['Competition-day checklist',[
'Before you leave: the board packed safely, a repair kit, notecards, professional attire, the conference schedule and a copy of the outline. At ILC: attend setup and the **Required Project Display Time**, check the display for damage, confirm compliance (size, no power, safety) and review your presentation.',
'In Round Two: arrive early, set up the display quickly, greet judges, present within five minutes and thank the judges. Afterward, reflect on what went well for next time.',
'Professional behavior throughout the conference reflects on your chapter.',
"After the event, keep your research and display. Many students later use the experience in college applications, job interviews and scholarship essays, and the career knowledge itself may guide their own path in health care.",
order('Competition day',['Pack the board, repair kit and notecards','Dress professionally','Attend required display time','Check display compliance','Present within five minutes','Thank the judges and reflect'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Final rehearsal',{a:{text:'In your final rehearsal, one member speaks for four minutes and the other for one. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Rebalance sections so both members present meaningful content','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing','b','Team participation is scored.',false]]},b:{text:'During Round Two, your teammate forgets the salary figure. What do you do?',choices:[['Smoothly add it while pointing to the chart','c','Correct.',true],['Correct them harshly','c','Support each other calmly.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team presents confidently, together and within time.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Delivery is 20 points: voice (pitch, tempo, volume), stage presence (poise, posture, eye contact, enthusiasm), diction (pronunciation, grammar) and team participation (5 each).','Speak clearly with varied pace and pauses, pronounce medical terms correctly, face judges while gesturing to the board and wear professional attire; both members share meaningful content with smooth handoffs.','Rehearse timed runs with feedback and video, manage nerves and follow a competition-day checklist—including the required display time and compliance check.'],terms:[['Diction','Clarity of pronunciation, grammar and articulation.'],['Stage presence','Poise, posture, eye contact and enthusiasm.'],['Handoff','A smooth transition from one presenter to the other.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is delivery worth in Round Two?','20','5','50','115','Four items at 5.'),q('Which item checks pronunciation and grammar?','Diction','Voice','Stage presence','Team participation','Articulation.'),q('Which item checks eye contact and posture?','Stage presence','Diction','Voice','Size','Poise.'),q('What should replace filler words?','Brief pauses','Louder fillers','Faster speech','Silence for a minute','Clarity.'),q('Team participation requires:','Both members taking an active role','One speaker only','The advisor speaking','Judges presenting','Teamwork.'),q('When gesturing to the board, you should:','Keep facing the judges','Turn your back','Block the board','Read it silently','Presence.'),q('Which helps manage nerves?','Slow breathing and practiced openings','Skipping rehearsal','Memorizing nothing','Arriving late','Preparation.'),q('What is the total for Round Two?','115 points','110 points','225 points','100 points','Round Two.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would prepare to earn full delivery and teamwork points in Round Two and what you would do on competition day.',rubric:[['Voice and diction',['volume','pitch','pace','pause','pronounce','grammar','filler']],['Stage presence',['eye contact','posture','gestures','enthusiasm','facing','attire']],['Teamwork',['both','equal','handoff','support','positions']],['Preparation',['rehearse','feedback','timed','video','checklist','display time','nerves']]],model:'Delivery is worth 20 points in Round Two—voice, stage presence, diction and team participation at five points each—so we would practice it deliberately. For voice, we would speak at a clear, comfortable volume, vary pitch and pace to emphasize key facts and pause instead of saying “um.” For diction, we would practice pronouncing terms such as bronchodilator and arterial blood gas correctly and use complete, grammatical sentences. For stage presence, we would stand tall, make eye contact with each judge, use natural gestures toward the board while still facing the judges, show enthusiasm for respiratory therapy and wear professional attire. For team participation, we would divide the content equally, with one of us leading the opening and responsibilities and the other leading education and salary, each sharing our own reason for choosing the career, and we would rehearse handoff lines and stand on opposite sides of the board. We would rehearse timed runs in front of classmates and our advisor with a one-minute card, video ourselves and use a feedback form to target weaknesses, and manage nerves with slow breathing and a well-practiced opening. On competition day, we would pack the board, a repair kit and notecards, attend the required display time to avoid the 15-point penalty, check size, safety and no-power compliance, present within five minutes and thank the judges.'},sources:[RULES,EHC]};
